New Features

Mobile push notifications

- End-to-end mobile push: iOS APNs token registration and Android FCM
- Rich-media notifications via the Notification Service Extension (ConnectNSE) and Notification Content Extension (ConnectNCE), rendering expanded content

Expo support (SDK 55+)

- Bundled Expo Config Plugin: provisions the ConnectNSE + ConnectNCE Xcode targets during `expo prebuild` (idempotent across re-runs, generated from the bundled NotificationViewController.swift template), adds the host-app `aps-environment` entitlement, and propagates `ConnectConfig.json` into the Android build — so APNs and FCM push work in Expo apps
- EAS Build support for the NSE + NCE extension targets, with a sample `eas.json` in `Examples/expo`
- Expo SDK 55+ sample app (`Examples/expo`) on expo-router, sharing the push/identity UI with the bare-workflow sample via `Examples/shared`

Bug Fixes

- Fix invalid AppKey shadowing: consumer's ConnectConfig.json AppKey now overrides the Connect AAR's baked-in default on Android
- Fix iOS runtime crash "Library not loaded: @rpath/EOCore.framework/EOCore" on newer merged Acoustic Connect SDK variants
- Fix Connect component breaking host-app touch handling when wrapped NavigationContainer has no ref
- Fix React 19 compatibility and constrain nitro-modules peer dep
- Fix ConnectConfig.json template
- Fix version update script for Android
- Expo: pass the dev-client URI scheme to `expo start` so the QR / deep link connects when the push NSE/NCE extensions are present
- bare-workflow: drop the CocoaPods git Specs source from the Podfile so a cold `pod install` no longer syncs the multi-GB Specs repo
- Android: restore the Tealeaf and EOCore classes on the compile classpath after the Connect Android SDK moved them to `runtime` scope, which stopped the bridge compiling
- Android: unwrap Nitro variant values in the bridge so signal and event payloads reach the native SDK with their original types instead of being flattened to strings
- Apply the `layout` block from `ConnectConfig.json` on both iOS and Android
- Apply advanced configuration to the file the Connect SDK actually reads, so advanced settings in `ConnectConfig.json` take effect
- Identity signals: the default method attribute now follows the signal type — `loggedIn` sends `loginMethod`, `accountRegistered` sends `registrationMethod`. Previously `loggedIn` signals defaulted to `registrationMethod` and were discarded by server-side schema validation
- Expo sample: resolve `react-native-worklets` through its prefab package so the Android build succeeds under Android Gradle Plugin 8, which relocated the CMake output directory. Sample-only — nothing here ships in the npm package
- Screen layout capture now honours `CaptureLayoutDelay` from `ConnectConfig.json`. The React Native wrapper previously always captured with no delay, so the setting had no effect on either platform and a capture triggered from React Navigation fired at the *start* of the screen transition, producing half-rendered layouts in session replay
- iOS: the delay argument to `logScreenLayout` is now milliseconds, matching Android and `CaptureLayoutDelay`. It was previously handed to the native API unconverted, where the unit is seconds — so a request for a 300 ms delay waited 300 seconds. If you passed a positive delay on iOS and compensated for this, drop the compensation

Improvements:

- New explicit `navigationRef` prop on <Connect> for ref passing without relying on children structure
- Rebuild SDK using nitro-modules 0.35.9
- Android: resolve react-native version conflicts from transitive Gradle deps
- iOS: verbose Connect/Tealeaf/EOCore native logging when `useRelease:false`, surfaced in the Xcode console for QA and support
- CI: Expo iOS + Android sample-build gates
- `logSignal` accepts nested JSON objects and arrays in `values`, so structured payloads no longer need flattening before they are sent
- `logSignal`: a top-level number in the payload now reaches the collector on Android as well as iOS, from Connect Android 11.0.24-beta — the version whose signal serializer gained a number branch. The Android dependency floats to the newest published build unless you pin it, so most integrations pick this up on their next build; nesting the number one level down stays portable if you pin an older one
- Sample apps: stable selectors on the demo screens for test automation
- Sample apps: `bare-workflow` and `expo` now ship a committed `Gemfile.lock`, and all sample Gemfiles hold `connection_pool` on the 2.x line so `bundle install` resolves on Ruby 3.1 as well as 3.3. Previously a cold `bundle install` picked up `connection_pool` 3.x (transitively, via `activesupport`), which requires Ruby >= 3.2 and aborted the install. Sample-only — nothing here ships in the npm package
- The default `CaptureLayoutDelay` is now 500 ms on both platforms, which clears a typical screen-transition animation. The shipped `ConnectConfig.json` templates carried 1 ms, and an integration with no layout block inherited each native SDK's own default — 1 ms on iOS against 500 ms on Android — so the two platforms captured at different points in the transition
- `TLTRN.logScreenLayout(name)` accepts an optional second argument to override the configured delay for a single call: `0` captures immediately, a positive value waits that many milliseconds. Useful for a screen whose content arrives asynchronously, where the capture is best triggered once the data is on screen rather than by raising the delay for every screen
- The npm package no longer ships the `SampleUI` demo app. It targeted React Native 0.76.9 — below this SDK's 0.82 floor — so it could not build against the package that carried it. This removes 326 files (~3.5 MB unpacked) from the tarball and changes nothing about the SDK itself. The maintained samples are `bare-workflow` (bare React Native) and `expo` (Expo SDK 55+), both published to the sample repository

Our environment for this release:

- Xcode 26.4.1
- MacOS 26.3
- iOS 15.1 to 26.x
- Supported architectures:
	- simulator
		- arm64
		- x86_64
	- device
		- arm64


